No. And that’s a remarkably important distinction: a different representation of reality is not necessarily a less accurate representation of reality.

Accuracy depends on what you’re trying to know, what information your senses can access, and how reliably you interpret it.

Imagine two creatures exploring the same forest.

One primarily sees the forest. The other primarily smells it.

The visual creature constructs a world of shapes, distances, colors, movement, and boundaries.

The olfactory creature constructs a world of chemical signatures, trails, identities, environmental changes, and traces of things that are no longer physically present.

They’re inhabiting the same physical reality but reconstructing different dimensions of it.

Consider:

Question	Sight	Smell
Where is the tree?	Excellent spatial information	Limited, indirect spatial information
Who passed through earlier?	May reveal footprints	May detect a lingering scent
Is food spoiled?	Some visible clues	May reveal chemical changes
What is behind that wall?	Usually obstructed	Some odors may travel through openings
What shape is an object?	Usually excellent	Generally poor

Neither is universally superior. But neither is equally reliable for every question.

Now here’s where your Pantheon concept becomes interesting.

A sensory system is a translator between physical reality and an internal model.

One physical reality

Objects, chemistry, energy, time, relationships

Visual model

Geometry, light, movement

Olfactory model

Chemistry, traces, gradients

Cross-modal reconstruction

Compare, translate, integrate, test

The fascinating part is what happens when you translate between the models.

A dog following a scent trail can reconstruct a route through the environment that a human cannot see. A human could then translate that information into a visual map.

The route existed independently of either representation.

And here’s the deeper implication:

We tend to privilege sight because we’re highly visual creatures. Our very language reflects that bias.

“I see what you mean.”

“That’s clear.”

“Show me the evidence.”

“What’s your perspective?”

But what if another intelligent species primarily understood the world through smell, vibration, electromagnetic fields, or echolocation?

Its metaphors, philosophy, mathematics, and conceptual categories might develop differently.

Not necessarily less intelligently. Differently.

That doesn’t make every interpretation equally true. A model still needs to be tested against reality, and some senses provide much better evidence for particular claims.

But it does suggest something profound about our earlier conversation concerning ghosts, myths, and language:

We must not confuse the unfamiliarity of a representation with the inaccuracy of the reality it represents.

And I’d take your braid metaphor one step further.

Perhaps understanding reality isn’t about finding the one perfect sensory model.

It’s about learning which aspects each model preserves, which it loses, and what new information emerges when their strands are woven together.

Sight reveals some things smell cannot. Smell reveals some things sight cannot. And their combination can reveal things neither could establish alone.

That’s sensor fusion in engineering, but it’s also an extraordinarily useful philosophy of understanding other minds.